WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:06.850 align:start position:0%
 
so the course I teach is a first-year  seminar called history of sexuality and  the approach that I take is I look at  the ways that scientists and physicians  over the past hundred years have tried  to study human sexuality so we go back  and we start with Richard from craft a  being who in the 1880s used examples  from his own psychiatric practice of  patients who are struggling with gender  identity or sexual orientation and I  should caution you that those are words  that we use to describe what he's  describing he used obviously a very  different set of words and part of our  interest is what is he interested in how  does he describe them he's very vivid in  describing the lives and experiences of  his patients and I think it's really  eye-opening because most of us naively  think that victorian-era  oh they didn't talk about sex oh they  didn't really do anything very out of  the normal sexually they were all sort  of married and having sex within  marriage but when you read his  descriptions of his patients you find  out that's very much not true and that  there was also a very self-conscious  understanding of what was going on both  on the part of the people engaged in  these activities and also emerging on  the part of the psychiatrists working on  this and then we keep moving forward we  look in the 1920s when anthropology  becomes really big in this field at  Malinowski's book called the sexual life  of savages where he goes to the  Trobriand islands and tries to  understand what is the sexual life of  this group of people living there and  basically what he finds as he finds that  they're much freer and easier with  sexuality that children are engaged in  sexual games that monogamy is not held  in as high esteem as it is in Europe in  around 1900 and he's very interested in  the freedom and the lack of difficulties  that this culture seems to offer in  comparison with his experience of  European culture we also then jump up to  Alfred Kinsey many of you may have heard  of the kinsey reports about five years  ago there was a Hollywood movie looking  at the production of the kinsey reports  and what Kinsey tried to do is do  very wide-ranging study of tens of  thousands of Americans in the 1940s  1950s about their sexual behaviors and a  number of the things that he found were  extremely shocking when he published  them but are sort of things we take for  granted now things like that most people  have sex before they get married at a  time when it was thought that no one had  sex before they got married that people  have same-sex relationships that women  have affairs while they're married which  no one thought was the case and we look  at his methods we read some of his texts  we also look at a slightly earlier  example of the study of women's  sexuality looking at the experience of  women who went to women's colleges in  1920s and whether or not they were still  happy if they married men so a lot of  these things are framed in the time  we're trying to understand the study of  human sexuality at the time it happens  so then when we get to the current  discussions of human sexuality I try to  encourage the students not to just say  oh now we have it right everybody who  worked on this in the past hundred years  weren't very good thinkers but now we  have it right the idea is that all of  our scientific studies of any subject  but particularly of sexuality are really  imbued with our cultural and social  notions so I want to give first-year  students no matter what kind of high  school they went to a really good  background and really good tools so  they're able to take on really  independent projects while they are at  Yale one of the first things we try to  take on is what happens when you're  assigned a really hard text to read and  we read two kinds of hard texts one is  these old scientific texts I mean for  those of you who aren't doing science a  contemporary scientific text is pretty  hard but then couple it with really old  ideas and really old fashioned language  and they get really hard so how to read  these old texts and define something  useful in things that your first  reaction is well they're wrong the  question then becomes interesting well  why did the major thinkers of that time  think this if it's so obviously wrong  and you get into a very nice discussion  the second kind of hard texts we read  are what is known as theory that is  people who  look at these primary texts if you will  and try to create a more overarching  understanding of say human sexuality so  the two theorists we read are Michele  Foucault and Freud and they're hard  because they make these very grand  statements and again have their own kind  of vocabulary and set of issues and in  both cases we spend several class  sessions on the readings to try to learn  how to really understand what they're  getting at so to go online with that we  write three different papers the first  paper is looking at a primary source  that is one of these physicians or  scientists writing about sexuality and  they have to pick two of them and  compare them on a set of issues that I  offer so how do you create a paper  that's based on close reading and  citation of a pretty hard primary text  that's the first paper the second paper  is how do you write about theory how do  you write about really smart people  writing about theoretical ideas and some  first-year students are really  intimidated by the idea of writing  comparing fucose ideas and Freud's ideas  when they maybe only understand them  partially and so we talk about how  that's kind of a standard of life that  maybe the portion of what you understand  these texts grows as you get further in  your education but it's always a good  idea to be somewhat humble in the face  of them and say I only understand part  of them and one thing I encourage  students to do is imagine you're  actually at a dinner party with these  two illustrious men and your goal as the  host or hostess is to try to keep the  dinner party conversation going without  having anybody come to blows so you ask  tossed questions out there that both  people will be interested in answering  but you want to keep the discussion  going and that's really your role as the  writer of a paper you want to keep the  discussion going between the two authors  you're trying to compare and then  finally the part of the class that many  students find to be their favorite is  they do a small original research paper  Yale is really lucky and that has a  tremendous archive on the subject of  sexuality actually we have several  different archives but the one we use is  the one in manuscripts and archives and  it's hard because the kinds of documents  that people have left in the past very  often were silent on the issue of  sexuality  but archivists and other scholars have  really gone through with a fine-tooth  comb and picked out for example letters  and large families letter collections  that refer obliquely to sexual behaviors  or sexual relationships or romantic  relationships and these are all flagged  already for us so we spend three weeks  in the archives actually really getting  students comfortable with working with  archival materials they frame their own  questions they pick their own objects  and they write a short research paper  and many of the students tell me that  that gives them the confidence after  that to sign up for classes that say  they have a research paper at the end as  many of our classes do and just to  conclude one of the things that many  students come into the course with is  they say they're taking the class  because they want to know whether  homosexuality is biological or cultural  and I smile and say I want to know that  too but the route I said ah you're not  at the end of the class gonna know the  answer to that because as far as I can  I'm concerned no one knows the answer to  that what you're gonna know is why that  question is so interesting to us and  when did that question become  interesting and why is it very  interesting to us now and many of the  students at the end of the class say  yeah I don't really want to know the  answer to that anymore but it's I really  realized what an interesting question it  is how much deeper of a question it is  than they thought when they started the  Freshman Seminar program at Yale is a  really awesome one because many  first-year students don't have the  opportunity to take a small  discussion-based course and if they do  manage to get into one they're the only  first-year student in a course of  juniors and seniors and it's pretty  scary frankly so the first year some of  our program offers the opportunity to be  in a seminar class of all other  first-year students taught by a faculty  member so there's a process for getting  into this there's a lottery that you  sign up for so you know I had last time  I thought this 144 students sign up for  my 18 spots and it was done by lottery  so I'm pretty sure there's not a real  way of saying who chose the class from  that means what it ends up with though  is a real genuine diversity of  backgrounds and when I go around and ask  students why they're taking it the main  reason that they give me is that  sexuality was something that wasn't  talked about in their families it wasn't  talked about in their churches and it  sure wasn't talked about in their  schools and  in fact they've never talked about it  and they're really wondering how you can  talk about it in a scholarly way in a  classroom setting and they just are  curious about anything I have to offer  so one of the benefits of the class too  is that people feel comfortable talking  about fairly frank sexual subjects not  about their own personal experiences but  about the cultures experience about the  ways that we represent other individuals  experiences the way that we understand  human sexuality as a concept so it's  really diverse in terms of background  one of the really interesting  backgrounds that people bring to my  class are when they come from other  countries in the world and I know I had  one student from Pakistan who took my  class and at the end was so excited he  said you know they don't study this at  universities in my country I'm going to  go back to Pakistan and found a  sexuality studies Department I'm like  yeah you go so there's a lot of  different perspectives and we heard a  lot from one student about the wisdom of  her Kenyan grandmother about human  sexuality and the advice that she gave  her so it's a it's great to have all the  different backgrounds in that class so I  also have a role I'm half of my position  at Yale I direct the office of LGBTQ  resources so I work in creating a  supportive environment for students  staff and faculty who may identify as  lesbian gay bisexual transgender or  queer on this campus so it's really nice  for me to also be in this classroom  setting where people may not have strong  identifications with those letters in  the alphabet or may or they may be  emerging and just having the opportunity  to talk about the history of where those  words came from the times that they were  used more by medical professionals to  label people the times that they were  more embraced by individuals with those  identities so I find also that the  students who are in my first-year  seminars I keep in touch with I always  run into them later on so many of them  become freshman counselors for example  partly because I think they've learned  how to be comfortable talking about some  difficult things maybe they already wear  somewhat comfortable talking about those  things they were they wouldn't have even  taken the class this year I was also  direct  undergraduate studies for WGS s and of  our seniors five of those seniors took  my first year seminar when they were  first year so it's been really wonderful  to see how they've grown and developed  and they come and tell me every time  they read Fuko sadly they don't read  Freud again mostly but they read  Foucault again and they'll come and talk  to me and say oh this time when I read  Foucault I really saw that point you  were making but I didn't get at all when  I was a first-year and it's just such a  pleasure to watch them grow and develop  and also to watch their comfort with the  archives  I think after they've done that archival  project so many of them go on and do to  archival projects for courses and for  their senior essays so it's just a  pleasure to to keep in touch with them  and watch them continue to grow and  change as they spend their four years at  you

